{"name":"Glossy Grape","hex":"#ab92b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glossy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a smooth, silklike, reflective (shiny) surface.","Attention-grabbing and superficially attractive."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glossy magazine.","A glossy photograph."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glossy"},{"term":"grape","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, round, smooth-skinned edible fruit, usually purple, red, or green, that grows in bunches on female vines of most species of genus Vitis and the self-fertile plants of species Vitis vinifera.","Any woody vine of genus Vitis that bears clusters of grapes; a grapevine."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing grapes or having a grape flavor.","Of a dark purplish red colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grape"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gloss (optics)","blocks":[{"text":"Gloss is an optical property which indicates how well a surface reflects light in a specular (mirror-like) direction. It is one of the important parameters that are used to describe the visual appearance of an object. Other categories of visual appearance related to the perception of regular or diffuse reflection and transmission of light have been organized under the concept of cesia in an order system with three variables, including gloss among the involved aspects. The factors that affect gloss are the refractive index of the material, the angle of incident light and the surface texture."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Gloss_light_Illuminates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gloss_(optics)","wikidata":"Q900581"},{"title":"Grape","blocks":[{"text":"A grape is a fruit, botanically a berry, of the deciduous woody vines of the flowering plant genus Vitis. Grapes are a non-climacteric type of fruit, generally occurring in clusters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Grapes%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg/330px-Grapes%2C_Rostov-on-Don%2C_Russia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grape","wikidata":"Q10978"},{"title":"Shades of violet","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is a color term derived from the flower of the same name. There are numerous variations of the color violet, a sampling of which are shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Color_icon_violet_v3.svg/330px-Color_icon_violet_v3.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_violet","wikidata":"Q17118741"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Nanorough.gif/330px-Nanorough.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Nanorough.gif/330px-Nanorough.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Figure1: Specular reflection of light from a rough surface","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANanorough.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Specular_gloss.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Specular_gloss.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpecular_gloss.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Sheen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Sheen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https